# Testing Required On Every Implementation

No feature, API, script, or UI change is done until tests exist and have been run.

## Required layers (add what fits; do not skip a layer without saying why)

| Layer | What it proves | Typical place |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unit | One function/class in isolation | `tests/unit/` or `*.test.ts` |
| Functional | User-facing behavior of one capability | `tests/functional/` |
| Integration | Modules/DB/API wired together | `tests/integration/` |
| Regression | Old bugs stay fixed; prior paths still pass | `tests/regression/` + re-run suite |
| Black-box | Behavior via public API/UI only (no internals) | `tests/blackbox/` |
| E2E | Real user journey end-to-end | `tests/e2e/` |

## Rules

- New code → new or updated tests in **each** applicable layer above.
- Bug fix → add a **regression** test that fails before the fix and passes after.
- Prefer the project's existing test runner (pytest / vitest / playwright / etc.). Do not add a new framework if one exists.
- Do not invent tests that never run. Wire them into the same commands the README documents.

## Self-verify before claiming done

1. Run the relevant test commands yourself.
2. Report pass/fail counts (or paste the summary).
3. If anything failed, fix it or say what blocked you — do not mark the task complete.
4. End with: what was tested, what was skipped and why, residual risk.
